.TH SORT 1
.SH NAME
sort \- sort lines
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B sort
[
.B \-nru
]
[
.I file ...
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Sort
sorts
lines of all the
.I files
together and writes the result on
the standard output.
If no input files are named, the standard input is sorted.
.PP
The sort key is an entire line.
The ordering is lexicographic by bytes.
Lines that compare equal come out in the order they went in.
The options are:
.TP
.B \-n
An initial numeric string,
consisting of optional blanks,
an optional minus sign,
and digits,
is sorted by arithmetic value.
A line with no such string sorts as 0.
Lines whose values are equal are ordered lexicographically.
.TP
.B \-r
Reverse the sense of comparisons.
.TP
.B \-u
Suppress all but one in each
set of identical lines.
.SH SOURCE
.B cmd/sort/main.odin
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR uniq (1)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
The exit status is empty, or
.B "can't open"
when a file could not be opened.
The other files are still sorted.
.SH BUGS
There are no fields, no
.BR \-f ,
no
.B \-o
and no
.BR \-m .
Under
.B \-u
lines are compared byte for byte, whatever the other options.
